# Snyk policy file. https://docs.snyk.io/manage-risk/policies/the-.snyk-file
version: v1.25.0

# .semgrep/ocm-sinks.php is not application code and never ships. It is the
# test fixture for the repository's own semgrep ruleset: every rule in
# .semgrep/ocm-sinks.yml has one deliberately vulnerable line in it and one
# safe line, and `semgrep --test` fails the build if a rule stops catching
# its vulnerable line. The Dockerfile copies only cms, cms-custom and
# errors into the image, so nothing in .semgrep/ is reachable at runtime.
#
# Snyk Code read that file as application source and reported the planted
# SQL injection and file inclusion as real findings. Excluding the
# directory hides nothing: a scanner fixture that stopped being vulnerable
# would be a broken fixture.
# tests/fixtures/ holds the fake services and harnesses the shell test suite
# runs against: zz_test_hibp.php is a stand-in for the Have I Been Pwned
# range API, and zz_test_ocm9_backports.php is a test runner that prints the
# exception from a failed assertion. Snyk Code read the first as an insecure
# hash and the second as a stack-trace leak. Neither is application code and
# the Dockerfile copies only cms, cms-custom and errors into the image.
exclude:
  global:
    - .semgrep/**
    - tests/fixtures/**

# Findings below are real sightings of md5 or sha1 that cannot be changed.
# Each one is either an algorithm another party picked, or a stored digest
# this code has to keep reading until the last row is migrated. They are
# listed here rather than left to fail the build, because the threshold is
# deliberately "low or higher" and a permanently red check tells nobody
# anything. Anything NOT listed here is expected to pass.
ignore:
  php/InsecureHash:
    - cms/app/lib/plPasswordBreach.php:
        reason: >-
          The Have I Been Pwned range API is defined in terms of SHA-1: the
          client sends the first five characters of the SHA-1 of the password
          and matches the returned suffixes. SHA-1 is the wire format, not a
          choice, and the digest is never stored. Sending anything else would
          simply return no results.
    - cms/services/twilio.php:
        reason: >-
          Twilio signs its webhooks with HMAC-SHA1 and sends the result in
          X-Twilio-Signature. Verifying the signature means computing the same
          HMAC-SHA1; any other algorithm would reject every genuine request.
          The comparison already uses hash_equals().
    - cms/app/lib/pikaCrypto.php:
        reason: >-
          TOTP is RFC 6238, whose default and near-universal algorithm is
          HMAC-SHA1. Authenticator apps generate codes that way, so the server
          has to check them that way. The secret is 20 bytes from
          random_bytes().
    - cms/app/lib/pikaAuthDb.php:
        reason: >-
          Legacy password migration. Rows created before password_hash() hold
          an md5 digest, so sign-in has to try md5 to let those users in at
          all. The match is hash_equals(), not ==, and a successful legacy
          match immediately rewrites the row with password_hash() and audits
          it as legacy_md5_upgraded, so each account passes through this path
          once and never again.
    - cms/app/lib/pl.php:
        reason: >-
          Same legacy digest as pikaAuthDb.php, read here during the
          password-plus-TOTP reauthentication check. password_verify() is
          tried first and md5 only as the fallback, through hash_equals().
    - cms/password.php:
        reason: >-
          The change-password form has to recognise the current password to
          accept a change, and for a not-yet-migrated account that means
          comparing the md5 digest on the row. Both comparisons use
          hash_equals(); the replacement password is written with
          password_hash().
    - cms/dataops.php:
        reason: >-
          The same current-password check as password.php, on the form-post
          path. hash_equals() against the stored digest, password_verify()
          first.
    - cms/ops/transfer_case.php:
        reason: >-
          The v5 transfer receiver rejects a packet with no "checksum" field,
          so the field stays for interoperability with deployments this code
          cannot upgrade. It is not the authenticity check: that is HTTP Basic
          plus the HMAC signature build_transfer_packet() adds.
  php/XxePrePHP8:
    - cms/ops/upload_report.php:
        reason: >-
          The rule covers PHP versions before 8.0, where libxml loaded
          external entities by default. This application supports PHP 8 only:
          the Dockerfile pins php:8.2-apache by digest and CI lints on 8.2.
          The call also passes LIBXML_NONET, so the parser cannot fetch a DTD
          or an entity over the network, and LIBXML_NOENT is deliberately not
          passed, so entity substitution stays off.
